// DeleteGovernancePolicyWithResponse Delete one governance policy.
//
// Deletes a policy this organization authored. A Manifold-maintained one is
// refused with 403 `policy_managed`.
//
// The policy stops being evaluated from the next event onward, and stops
// appearing in the catalog. What it already produced stays: the violations it
// caught record what happened, and deleting the rule does not unmake those
// events.
//
// Its audit trail also survives, at the same address — `GET
// /governance/policies/{policyId}/audit-logs` keeps answering for a deleted
// policy, and the deletion is the last entry on it. That is deliberate: the
// record of a policy going away is the one thing you would most want to read
// afterwards, and requiring the cross-domain feed for it would mean needing
// `read:audit-logs` to learn what happened to your own policy.
//
// Deleting is idempotent only in effect, not in status: a second delete of
// the same id is a 404, since the policy is no longer there to delete.
//
// ## Concurrency
//
// Send `If-Match` with the `ETag` from the read this delete is based on and
// the policy goes only if nobody has changed it since; a stale version is
// refused with 412 and the policy stays. Without the header the delete is
// unconditional.
